## 14. The pitch: 8 minutes plus 2

One presenter speaks; a second person drives the screen. At most five numbers in the whole talk. Slides carry about seven words each; the graphics carry the rest. The deck is HTML in the theme (Section 14.3), presented from the laptop, with the PPTX and PDF handed to the organiser.

| Minute | Slide | On screen | Spoken line (the gist) |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| 0:00 | 1. A receipt | One real posting sentence, its employer, its date, and "1 of 24 reviewed postings" | "This is a receipt. By the end of this talk you will have seen a course decision made of them." The sentence from Section 2.3. |
| 0:30 | 2. The latency | Two bars: approval 12 to 18 months; 39% of skills change by 2030 | The world problem, one figure, one caveat ("they describe different populations") |
| 1:15 | 3. Who it lands on | Dr Aisyah's desk: tabs, a syllabus, a message asking what to change | Fictional lecturer, real workflow |
| 1:45 | 4. The loop | The six steps with the seven mascots | Watch, Report, Decide, Update, Teach, Learn: the same names in the app you are about to see |
| 2:15 | 5. Live: Watch and Report | The app (or the 90-second film if the room is hostile): fetch, confirm a quotation, watch a paraphrase fail, the count with its denominator | "Nothing invented, nothing pasted. A paraphrase is rejected." |
| 4:15 | 6. Decide and Update | The brief with receipts, the decision record, the checked revision | "The decision stays with people, and it leaves a record" |
| 5:00 | 7. Teach and Learn | The teaching pack opened in PowerPoint; the Learn screen with n of N and the gap | "No model wrote a word of this pack" |
| 5:45 | 8. What is real | Software TRL 6 in NASA's words; the checks; the sample; what is not built, in amber | Honest limits; the TRL 7 step named: one department, one term, inside its LMS |
| 6:15 | 9. The doors | The architecture diagram | "One core; the assistants, the LMS and the office suite they already run; local read-only door built, remote planned" |
| 6:50 | 10. Why a department pays | The ladder and the accreditor clauses | "The review happens anyway; the pack writes itself" |
| 7:20 | 11. The ask and the close | The pilot measures; the three points restated; the sentence | "Task completion, citation fidelity, time to a brief, repeat use. Evidence that arrives with a decision." |

Questions bank for the two minutes: is this scraping; where is the AI; why not ChatGPT alone; how do we know the postings are representative; what breaks first at scale; does it decide the syllabus; why another tool; what does the free tier cost us; what is the benchmark; is lime readable. Each answer is one breath and ends with what is on screen.

### 14.1 Delivery

Open with the receipt, not with "hi, we are team". Pause after the paraphrase is rejected. Say the limits slower than the claims. Never read a slide. Restate three points at the end: receipts, people decide, the tools they already use.

### 14.2 Slide craft

One message per slide; titles as full sentences; four lines of text at most; big numbers in Bricolage; real screenshots with a hairline border; mascots where they serve a purpose; the amber token for everything not built; no stock imagery, no gradients, no logos in colour.

### 14.3 Production

`deck/` in the repo: an HTML deck at 1920 by 1080 using the tokens, the bundled fonts, the mascots and real screenshots from `scripts/presentation-shots.mjs`, with keyboard navigation and the film embedded as a local file. `scripts/gen-deck.mjs` exports a PDF and a PPTX (one image per slide at 2x plus the speaker notes as real notes, built with the pptxgenjs dependency already in the project). Present from the HTML (animations and the film run offline); hand over the PPTX and the PDF. The brief PDF is regenerated from the same tokens.

