# Research note: statistics for Lyceum

Date: 22 October 2026. The owner's question: can a survey of a few students stand for the whole cohort, and can the CLO survey and the marks together predict acceptance and expected mastery, quantitative and qualitative at once? This note names the standard method for each part, what it needs, and when Lyceum must say "unknown". References were checked in a research session on 22 October 2026 against publisher or DOI records; class-size figures are computed from the cited formulas.

## 1. Sample size to represent a finite population

- **Cochran with the finite population correction.** n0 = z²p(1 - p)/e², then n = n0 / (1 + (n0 - 1)/N). With p = 0.5, 95% confidence, ±5 points: n0 = 385. Krejcie and Morgan (1970) tabulate the same arithmetic. The rule bounds sampling error only; it needs random sampling, or a census with random non-response. Cochran, Sampling Techniques, 3rd ed., Wiley. Krejcie and Morgan, EPM 30(3), 607-610. https://doi.org/10.1177/001316447003000308
- **What a class needs at ±5 points, 95%:** N = 40 needs 36 or 37 responses (90% of the class); N = 120 needs 92 (77%); N = 300 needs 169 (56%). With 20 of 40 responding, the corrected margin on a proportion is about ±16 points; 60 of 120 gives ±9; 100 of 300 gives ±8.
- **A census beats a sample for small classes.** Israel (University of Florida IFAS) calls a census attractive below roughly 200. https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/PD006. Nulty (2008) says the same for course evaluations, with a liberal standard (80%, ±10) and a stringent one (95%, ±3). Assessment and Evaluation in Higher Education 33(3), 301-314. https://doi.org/10.1080/02602930701293231
- **Non-response bias (Groves).** Bias = non-response rate × (responders minus non-responders on the measured thing): a low rate only hurts when the two groups differ, and a high rate is no guarantee. Groves 2006, POQ 70(5). https://doi.org/10.1093/poq/nfl033. Groves and Peytcheva 2008, 59 studies, POQ 72(2). https://doi.org/10.1093/poq/nfn011. Practice: compare responders with the full class on marks and show the difference.

## 2. Interval estimates for small samples

- **Wilson score** for proportions. Centre (p̂ + z²/2n)/(1 + z²/n), half-width z/(1 + z²/n) × sqrt(p̂(1 - p̂)/n + z²/4n²). Wilson 1927, JASA 22(158). https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1927.10502953
- **Agresti-Coull.** Add two successes and two failures, then the plain interval. The American Statistician 52(2). https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1998.10480550
- **Which one.** Brown, Cai and DasGupta: never Wald; Wilson or Jeffreys (Beta(x + ½, n - x + ½) quantiles) for n ≤ 40, Agresti-Coull above. Statistical Science 16(2), 101-133. https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1009213286. Keep Lyceum's Wilson, times sqrt((N - n)/(N - 1)) for a census with non-response.
- **t-interval for means** (1 to 10 ratings, SSRT): mean ± t(n - 1) × s/√n. Student 1908, Biometrika 6(1). https://www.jstor.org/stable/2331554
- **Bootstrap** (percentile or BCa) for medians and CLO-weighted marks: resample the n students, B ≥ 1,000; unreliable near n = 10. Efron 1979, Annals of Statistics 7(1). https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552

## 3. Borrowing strength for small cohorts

- **Multilevel model, partial pooling.** A cohort's per-CLO estimate is a precision-weighted blend of its own mean and the programme or past-cohort mean: θ̂_j ≈ (n_j ȳ_j/σ² + μ/τ²)/(n_j/σ² + 1/τ²). Needs several comparable groups (sections, past semesters) to estimate the between-group variance. Gelman and Hill 2007, ch. 12. http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/arm/. Empirical Bayes: Efron and Morris 1975, JASA 70(350). Small-area estimation adds covariates: Rao and Molina 2015, Wiley.
- **What it buys a 25-student cohort.** A raw CLO proportion at n = 25 has a Wilson half-width near ±19 points; pooled with three earlier cohorts it typically lands near ±10. Caveat: shrinkage hides a real break unless the redesign is a covariate, so always show raw beside pooled.

## 4. Measuring mastery

- **Rasch (1PL) and 2PL IRT.** P(correct) = logistic(θ - b) or logistic(a(θ - b)). Minimum calibration samples (Linacre 1994, Rasch Measurement Transactions 7(4), 328, https://www.rasch.org/rmt/rmt74m.htm): 30 persons for item difficulty within ±1 logit at 95%; 100 for ±0.5 logit; 250 or more for high-stakes use; polytomous items need 10 observations per category. 2PL needs 150 to 750 depending on test length (Şahin and Anil 2017, https://open.metu.edu.tr/handle/11511/65131); 3PL needs thousands. A 40-student semester cannot calibrate items: the Rasch spine stays illustrative until items are pooled across cohorts.
- **Bayesian Knowledge Tracing.** A hidden Markov model per skill; needs attempt sequences per student per skill, not end-of-semester marks. Corbett and Anderson 1995. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01099821
- **Mastery learning (Bloom 1968).** Over 90% of students can reach mastery when time and feedback vary; practice uses 80 to 90% on a criterion-referenced test. https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED053419
- **Standard setting.** Angoff: each judge estimates the probability that a minimally competent student answers each item; the sum is the cut score. Bookmark: items ordered by difficulty, judges mark where a borderline student's chance drops below about 0.67. Cizek and Bunch 2007, SAGE. Panel size 10 to 15 judges, from Hurtz and Hertz 1999, EPM 59(6). https://doi.org/10.1177/00131649921970233
- **Umbrella standard.** AERA, APA and NCME, Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (2014): validity, reliability and fairness for a stated use. https://www.testingstandards.net/

## 5. Self-reported mastery against assessed attainment

- Sitzmann, Ely, Brown and Bauer 2010 (meta-analysis): self-assessed knowledge tracks motivation and satisfaction more than measured learning; the learning link is only moderate. AMLE 9(2). https://doi.org/10.5465/AMLE.2010.51428542
- Kruger and Dunning 1999: bottom-quartile performers overrate themselves. https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.77.6.1121. Gignac and Zajenkowski 2020: much of that pattern is regression to the mean. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intell.2020.101449
- Uttl, White and Gonzalez 2017: no meaningful correlation between student ratings of teaching and learning once small-study bias is removed. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.stueduc.2016.08.007
- Practice: report perception (self-rating, SSRT) and attainment (CLO-mapped marks) as separate series with intervals and show the gap; never average them into one mastery number.

## 6. Predicting the effect of a course change

- **Naive baseline.** Next cohort = last cohort; simple methods "serve as benchmarks rather than the method of choice". Hyndman and Athanasopoulos, FPP3, ch. 5. https://otexts.com/fpp3/simple-methods.html
- **Difference-in-differences.** (changed course after minus before) minus (comparison course after minus before); needs an unchanged comparison course and a cohort each side; assumes parallel trends. Card and Krueger 1994. https://www.jstor.org/stable/2118030
- **Interrupted time series.** Segmented regression over a run of cohorts; needs several cohorts on each side. Bernal, Cummins and Gasparrini 2017. https://doi.org/10.1093/ije/dyw098
- **Pre-registration.** Freeze the predicted numbers, metric and analysis before the semester's data arrive. Nosek et al 2018, PNAS. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1708274114
- **Calibration.** Brier score (1/N) Σ (f - o)²; reliability diagram (Murphy and Winkler 1977, https://doi.org/10.2307/2346866); maximise sharpness subject to calibration (Gneiting, Balabdaoui and Raftery 2007, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2007.00587.x).
- **"Acceptance" as a construct.** Technology Acceptance Model: perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, two validated six-item scales (Davis 1989, MIS Quarterly 13(3), https://doi.org/10.2307/249008). Kirkpatrick's levels separate Reaction from Learning; Alliger et al 1997: reactions are only weakly related to learning. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1744-6570.1997.tb00911.x

## 7. Combining qualitative and quantitative

- **Thematic analysis**, six phases. Braun and Clarke 2006. https://doi.org/10.1191/1478088706qp063oa
- **Inter-rater reliability.** Cohen's κ. https://doi.org/10.1177/001316446002000104. Landis and Koch bands are convention, not evidence. Protocol (double-code a subset, report κ, reconcile the codebook): O'Connor and Joffe 2020. https://doi.org/10.1177/1609406919899220
- **Integration.** Convergent mixed-methods design (Creswell and Plano Clark 2017); triangulation protocol with a convergence matrix per theme (O'Cathain, Murphy and Nicholl 2010, BMJ 341:c4587, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.c4587).
- **LLM coding of open comments.** Pangakis, Wolken and Fasching 2023: performance is "highly contingent" on the task, so every run must be validated against human labels. https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00176. Törnberg 2024 gives the protocol: codebook, structured prompts, stability checks, validation. https://doi.org/10.6092/issn.1971-8853/19461. A 2025 study: only a fine-tuned model reached macro-F1 0.87, and models mishandle "don't know" codes. https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.14634. Lyceum protocol: a human gold set of 100 to 200 comments, κ shown in the UI, human sign-off, re-validation on any prompt or model change.

## 8. Standards and ethics

- JISC Code of Practice for Learning Analytics: responsibility, transparency and consent, privacy, validity, access, minimising adverse impacts, stewardship. https://www.jisc.ac.uk/guides/code-of-practice-for-learning-analytics
- DELICATE checklist, Drachsler and Greller 2016. https://doi.org/10.1145/2883851.2883893. Prinsloo and Slade 2017, Handbook of Learning Analytics ch. 4. https://doi.org/10.18608/hla17.004
- GDPR Article 22: a right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing with significant effects. https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/. A per-student predicted mastery that triggers an intervention needs meaningful human review; cohort-level predictions do not engage it.
- Malaysia PDPA 2010 (Act 709), seven principles; the 2024 amendment adds a data protection officer duty, breach notification and portability, with no Article 22-style right found. https://www.pdp.gov.my/. Treat per-student predictions as profiling anyway.

## Recommended stack for Lyceum

| cohort | survey | per-CLO estimate | mastery calibration | prediction | label unknown when |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| under 60 | census; show n of N; FPC-adjusted Wilson and t | partial pooling with past cohorts, raw beside pooled | none; the Rasch spine stays "illustrative" | naive baseline only | responses below the Krejcie-Morgan figure; fewer than 2 prior cohorts |
| 60 to 200 | census target; Nulty stringent standard | same, pooled across sections | Rasch at 100+ responses per item, stable at 150+; no 2PL | difference-in-differences with an unchanged comparison course | no comparison course; fewer than 3 cohorts |
| over 200, or semesters pooled | Agresti-Coull is fine | hierarchical over cohort × CLO | 2PL at 500+ with unchanged items; Angoff or Bookmark cut score, 10 to 15 judges | interrupted time series over 6+ cohorts, pre-registered, Brier-scored | under about 30 resolved predictions |

Always: perception and attainment shown separately with the gap; predictions frozen before data and scored against carry-forward; comments coded against a human gold set with κ shown; no solely automated per-student decisions; JISC and DELICATE as written policy.

===== FILE: docs/research/doors-and-taxonomies-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: the doors, the skill taxonomies, the boards and the standards

Date: 22 October 2026. For the "one core, many doors" architecture: what the assistants universities run can connect to today, what the open Agent Skills standard makes possible, which skill taxonomies are free, which job boards have a documented public API, and which professional curriculum standards can stand as supply-side references. Facts were fetched from official pages in a research session on 22 October 2026 unless marked unverified.

## 1. MCP support in the assistants universities run

- **Copilot Studio.** MCP generally available since 29 May 2025 (Add a tool, search MCP server; generative orchestration required; Streamable HTTP, SSE deprecated). https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot/blog/copilot-studio/model-context-protocol-mcp-is-now-generally-available-in-microsoft-copilot-studio/. MCP tools in agent workflows generally available 15 July 2026. Licensing: needs the standalone Copilot Studio subscription with Copilot Credits; the Teams plan bundled in Microsoft 365 has no generative orchestration, so no MCP. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/requirements-licensing-subscriptions
- **Microsoft 365 Copilot declarative agents.** MCP plugins are custom actions inside declarative agents; tools discovered at runtime; packaged with the Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/extensibility/overview-plugins. Agents go through submission and approval in the admin centre Agent Registry. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/admin/manage/manage-copilot-agents-integrated-apps. The Copilot add-on is sold on Microsoft 365 A1, A3 and A5 for faculty, staff and students aged 13 and over. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/copilot-license-options
- **ChatGPT.** Apps SDK announced 6 October 2025, built on MCP; launch apps included Coursera and Canva; preview for Business, Enterprise and Edu from 13 November 2025. https://openai.com/index/introducing-apps-in-chatgpt/. Admin turns on Developer mode, only owners and admins publish; Enterprise and Edu add role-based access. https://help.openai.com/en/articles/12584461-build-with-the-apps-sdk. Public directory: verified developer identity, OAuth or no-auth only, no API-key flow. https://developers.openai.com/apps-sdk/deploy/submission
- **Claude.** Custom connectors (remote MCP) on Free, Pro, Max, Team and Enterprise; on Team and Enterprise the Owner adds it under Organization settings, then each member authenticates. https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166-get-started-with-custom-connectors-using-remote-mcp. Claude for Education (2 April 2025) added a Canvas LTI and pre-built MCP connectors for Panopto and Wiley on 9 July 2025. https://www.anthropic.com/news/advancing-claude-for-education
- **Google.** Gemini Enterprise supports a "custom MCP server" data store; the admin must override the policy that blocks custom MCP stores, allow-list domains and register an OAuth client; Streamable HTTP only, public-CA TLS. https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini/enterprise/docs/connectors/custom-mcp-server/set-up-custom-mcp-server. Consumer and Workspace Gemini app custom MCP: not confirmed. Gemini CLI extensions bundle MCP servers from any GitHub repo.

## 2. Agent Skills and comparable formats

- Claude Skills launched 16 October 2025 (Pro, Max, Team, Enterprise; Claude Code; API); 18 December 2025 added organisation-wide management and a partner skills directory. https://claude.com/blog/skills
- Open standard: SKILL.md with YAML front matter (name and description required; license, compatibility, metadata, allowed-tools optional) plus scripts, references and assets folders. https://agentskills.io/specification. Repository Apache-2.0 code, CC-BY-4.0 docs. https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills
- Client showcase lists 46 products: Claude, Claude Code, ChatGPT and Codex, GitHub Copilot, VS Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor, OpenCode, OpenHands, Goose, Kiro, Databricks, Snowflake, JetBrains Junie, Tabnine, Qodo, Roo Code, Mistral Vibe and others. https://agentskills.io/clients
- OpenAI: ChatGPT and Codex skills "build on the open agent skills standard"; distribution is a plugin (skills plus MCP plus optional UI) in the ChatGPT directory. https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/build-skills
- Microsoft: Copilot Studio agents accept uploaded skill files or packages (9 September 2026). https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/agents-experience/skills-overview. GitHub Copilot reads .github/skills, .claude/skills and .agents/skills. https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/about-agent-skills
- Google: Gemini CLI supports the standard (`gemini skills install`). https://geminicli.com/docs/cli/skills/
- Verdict: a product can ship its workflow as one skill folder and it loads unchanged in Claude, ChatGPT and Codex, Copilot Studio, GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI.

## 3. LMS integration standards

- LTI 1.3 Core plus LTI Advantage: Deep Linking 2.0, Names and Role Provisioning 2.0, Assignment and Grade Services 2.0, on OAuth 2.0, OIDC and JWT. https://standards.1edtech.org/lti/specifications/guides/implementation_guide/implementation-guide
- QTI 3.0 final 11 May 2022. https://www.1edtech.org/standards/qti/versions. Moodle XML quiz format. https://docs.moodle.org/en/Moodle_XML_format
- Canvas: LTI 1.3 configuration through Developer Keys; Dynamic Registration lets an admin paste one URL. https://developerdocs.instructure.com/services/canvas/external-tools/lti/file.lti_dev_key_config. Moodle: Site administration, Plugins, External tool, Manage tools, paste the registration URL. https://help.feedbackfruits.com/hc/en-us/articles/23527115832082-How-to-configure-LTI-1-3-for-Moodle
- 1EdTech certification is bundled with paid Alliance membership; prices not published. https://www.1edtech.org/certification/lti
- Teams for Education: Graph education APIs (rostering, assignments, submissions) with admin consent. https://learn.microsoft.com/graph/education-concept-overview. Google Classroom add-ons: Workspace Marketplace, Teaching and Learning or Plus licences, domain allow-list. https://developers.google.com/classroom/add-ons

## 4. Open skill taxonomies and APIs

- **ESCO (EU).** 3,039 occupations, 13,939 skills, 28 languages; v1.2.1 (10 December 2025); free download in CSV, JSON-LD, RDF and XML; hosted web API and a local API (EUPL 1.2). Reuse under CC BY 4.0 with attribution. https://esco.ec.europa.eu/en/use-esco/download and https://esco.ec.europa.eu/en/use-esco/use-esco-services-api
- **O*NET (US).** Database 31.0 under CC BY 4.0; Web Services free with registration and attribution; paid applications need written permission. https://www.onetcenter.org/database.html and https://services.onetcenter.org/help/license_data
- **Lightcast Open Skills.** 33,000 or more skills; the free tier is for non-profit and public-good work only (50 extractions a month, by approval); "A commercial license is required for for-profit or production use cases." https://docs.lightcast.io/lightcast-api/docs/api-access
- **Singapore.** Sector Skills Frameworks on skillsfuture.gov.sg; course datasets on data.gov.sg under the Open Data Licence; the SSG developer portal listed no APIs when fetched. https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/skills-framework/ict
- **UK.** Skills England dashboards under OGL v3.0; the DfE and IfATE Standard Skills Classification is a project, not a published dataset. https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/job-and-skills-data
- **Australia.** The Australian Skills Classification was decommissioned (removed January 2025); the National Skills Taxonomy is in consultation. https://www.jobsandskills.gov.au/work/australian-skills-classification
- **Malaysia.** MyMahir (TalentCorp) lists 800 or more skills and links to the Critical Occupations List; no API or licence stated. https://mymahir.my/
- **Nesta.** ojd_daps_skills maps advert text to ESCO or Lightcast, MIT licence; data releases stopped in 2022. https://github.com/nestauk/ojd_daps_skills
- **ILO ISCO-08.** Four-level occupation classification, free to use without prior authorisation.

## 5. Legitimate job-posting sources

- **Ashby.** GET api.ashbyhq.com/posting-api/job-board/{board}, no credentials shown, full descriptions. https://developers.ashbyhq.com/docs/public-job-posting-api
- **Workable.** Public endpoint api/accounts/:subdomain returns an account's public jobs. https://workable.readme.io/
- **SmartRecruiters.** Posting API for customer career sites; per-company postings reported keyless where the customer enables it (unverified). https://developers.smartrecruiters.com/docs/posting-api
- **Workday.** No documented public API; treat as unavailable.
- **Adzuna.** REST API with app id and key by registration; quotas in the registration terms. https://developer.adzuna.com/overview
- **USAJOBS.** Free key; public data may be copied, analysed and redistributed, attribution encouraged. https://developer.usajobs.gov/guides/terms-of-service
- **EURES.** No documented public API found; do not rely on it.
- **Indeed.** Publisher API retired 2023; terms forbid scraping. https://www.indeed.com/legal. **LinkedIn.** User Agreement 8.2 bans scraping and bots. https://www.linkedin.com/legal/user-agreement
- **schema.org JobPosting.** Google requires datePosted, description, hiringOrganization, jobLocation and title on single-job pages; employer career pages that publish it are a legitimate structured source. https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/job-posting

## 6. Supply-side curriculum standards

- CS2023 (ACM, IEEE-CS, AAAI), January 2024. https://csed.acm.org/
- ACM Computing Competencies for Undergraduate Data Science Curricula, 2021; a successor is being presented in 2026. https://dstf.acm.org/
- SFIA 9, 30 October 2024; free for internal and personal use, a fee-bearing Partner Licence to sell products that use SFIA. https://sfia-online.org/en/about-sfia/licensing-sfia
- NICE Cybersecurity Workforce Framework, NIST SP 800-181r1 plus components in JSON. https://www.nist.gov/itl/applied-cybersecurity/nice/nice-framework-resource-center/nice-framework-current-versions
- CDIO Syllabus 3.0 and Standards 3.0. https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/534193
- IEA Graduate Attributes and Professional Competencies v4 (Washington Accord). https://www.internationalengineeringalliance.org/accords/washington-accord
- AACSB 2026 Global Standards (Standards 4, 5 and 6). https://www.aacsb.edu/educators/accreditation/business-accreditation/aacsb-business-accreditation-standards. EQUIS Standards. https://www.efmdglobal.org/accreditations/business-schools/equis/

## 7. The doors are real: examples, 2024 to 2026

- Coursera shipped as an app inside ChatGPT at the Apps SDK launch, 6 October 2025.
- Claude for Education: Canvas LTI plus Panopto and Wiley MCP connectors, 9 July 2025.
- University of Hong Kong: a first-year undergraduate Copilot built in Copilot Studio (Microsoft customer story, 27 January 2025). https://www.microsoft.com/en/customers/story/21598-the-university-of-hong-kong-microsoft-365-copilot
- Florida State University faculty course assistants in Copilot Studio (29 August 2024). https://its.fsu.edu/node/15491. Teesside University staff guide to module agents (July 2025). https://eatsupport.tees.ac.uk/staff/knowledgebase/building-ai-agents-with-microsoft-copilot/
- EduBase (assessment platform) ships a remote MCP server for claude.ai (2026). https://www.edubase.net/news/feed/51/Connect-EduBase-with-Claude-in-Under-a-Minute-The-Complete-MCP-Integration-Guide-2026-Update
- Canvas and Moodle MCP servers exist as community projects on personal API tokens; no official MCP repository under instructure, moodle or openedx on 22 October 2026.

## What this means for Lyceum's architecture

1. One remote MCP server (Streamable HTTP, HTTPS with a public CA, OAuth 2.0, or no-auth for read-only tools) is the single door into Claude, ChatGPT, Copilot Studio, Microsoft 365 Copilot declarative agents and Gemini Enterprise.
2. Do not build SSE; Copilot Studio and Gemini Enterprise have dropped it, and ChatGPT's directory has no API-key path.
3. Ship a Lyceum skill folder (SKILL.md plus scripts) under the open standard; the same files load in Claude, Codex and ChatGPT plugins, Copilot Studio, GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI.
4. Package the same core as a ChatGPT plugin and as a Microsoft 365 declarative agent, and publish per-vendor admin steps, because every door is admin-gated.
5. The LMS door is LTI 1.3 with Deep Linking (push packs into Canvas and Moodle) plus QTI 3.0 and Moodle XML exports for quizzes; 1EdTech certification waits for revenue.
6. Key quotations to ESCO (CC BY 4.0, 28 languages) with an O*NET crosswalk and ISCO-08 codes; keep Lightcast optional because commercial use needs a licence.
7. Sources stay Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workable, SmartRecruiters, USAJOBS and Adzuna, plus schema.org JobPosting from employer career pages; never LinkedIn, Indeed or Workday feeds.
8. Supply-side references (CS2023, SFIA 9, NICE, CDIO, Washington Accord, AACSB 2026) give the lecturer a legitimate baseline for what a course should contain.
9. Put licences on the diagram: Copilot Studio needs its own subscription; Lightcast and SFIA commercial use need licences; ESCO, O*NET and USAJOBS are free with attribution.
10. The proof for judges that the doors are real: Coursera inside ChatGPT, Wiley and Panopto inside Claude for Education, HKU's agent inside Copilot Studio.

===== FILE: docs/research/hackastone-and-competitors-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: HackaSTONE, readiness levels, pitch evidence, the market and the competitors

Date: 22 October 2026. For the Grand Final: what the competition is, how to state readiness honestly, what judges reward, who buys in universities, who else sells alignment, and what the education evidence allows us to claim. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026; sources marked vendor or secondary where they are.

## 1. What HackaSTONE is

- Official site hackastone.varteledu.com. Organiser: the Association of Global AI Technomics, Education and Exchange (GATEE). Theme "Agentic AI for Education": AI assistants for higher education, K-12, preschool and special needs, vocational and lifelong learning. https://hackastone.varteledu.com/
- Leadership: advisor Chris Dede (Harvard); general chairs Yiyu Cai (NTU), Simon See (NVIDIA), Sui Lin Goei (Windesheim); technical committee co-chairs Wouter van Joolingen (Utrecht) and Qi Cao (NUS). https://hackastone.varteledu.com/steering-committee/
- Scale and stages: over 50 universities, about 20 countries, over 150 teams; finalists 15 August; winners 29 October 2026 (ITS news, secondary). https://www.its.ac.id/?p=72149
- Grand Final: 29 October 2026, Amsterdam, inside "AI for Education The Netherlands 2026" (VU Amsterdam day one, UvA and HvA day two). Format: "max 8 min project presentation", "2 mins QnA", then "max 10 mins demonstration including QnA" at a team booth. Prizes: first S$1000 (up to 5 winners), second S$600 (up to 10), third S$350 (up to 15), merit S$200 (up to 20). Teams are strongly encouraged to bring university T-shirts and banners. https://hackastone.varteledu.com/grand-final/
- Published criteria, four, no weights on the site: Innovation and Novelty ("original thought, creative ingenuity, and a meaningful departure" from existing solutions); Technical Readiness Level ("maturity, robustness, and operational functionality of the implementation", "demonstrable performance"); Commercial Viability ("credible path to adoption"); Educational Significance and Applicability ("contribution to learning outcomes and its capacity for meaningful deployment"). https://hackastone.varteledu.com/spotlight/. The briefing slide photographed on 22 October shows Innovation 30%, Technology Readiness 20%, Commercial Viability 15%, Education Significance 15%, Presentation 10%, Demo 10%, marked "to be firmed"; confirm against the finalist pack.
- Finalists announced 12 to 14 October 2026 include EmoAgent (SCNU, social-emotional dialogue agent), Charlie humanoid tutor (SUSS), Signify (ITB, deaf learners), Cognitive Arena (UESTC), Cogniva (ITB, learning by teaching), EcoLearn (BVRIT) and EduVent (ITS). Nearly all are learner-facing tutors; a lecturer-facing decision tool is a positioning gap. https://hackastone.varteledu.com/news-update/
- Past winners: none found; this looks like the first edition.

## 2. Technology Readiness Level, adapted to software

- EU wording (Horizon 2020 Annex G, carried into Horizon Europe): TRL 5 "technology validated in relevant environment"; TRL 6 "technology demonstrated in relevant environment"; TRL 7 "system prototype demonstration in operational environment"; TRL 8 "system complete and qualified"; TRL 9 "actual system proven in operational environment". https://ec.europa.eu/research/participants/data/ref/h2020/wp/2014_2015/annexes/h2020-wp1415-annex-g-trl_en.pdf
- NASA ESTO software column: TRL 5 "End-to-end software elements implemented and interfaced with existing systems/simulations"; TRL 6 "Prototype implementations of the software demonstrated on full-scale realistic problems", partially integrated, limited documentation; TRL 7 "Prototype software exists having all key functionality available for demonstration and test", integrated with operational systems; TRL 8 fully debugged, documentation complete, verification done; TRL 9 run in the operational environment. https://esto.nasa.gov/trl/
- NASA reviewer checklist of weak TRL claims: "The 'relevant' environment is not defined" and "Software development is not addressed". Define the relevant environment: real postings, a real syllabus, a real lecturer. https://lws.larc.nasa.gov/vfmo/pdf_files/Common-causes-TRL-Weaknesses-Ver03.080118.pdf
- Honest placement for Lyceum: software TRL 6. TRL 7 needs one department running it for a term inside its LMS; TRL 8 needs full documentation and verification.
- EdTech evidence ladders: Digital Promise "Research-Based Design" (ESSA Tier 4) needs a logic model with five citations, an annotated bibliography of five design decisions and one qualitative user study; "Evidence-Based Edtech" (Tier 3) needs a well-designed quantitative study showing benefits for learners. https://digitalpromise.org/product-certifications/
- US Department of Education letter, 20 August 2026: judge edtech on outcomes, not usage; "Responsible design is the floor". https://www.edsurge.com/news/department-of-education-issues-long-awaited-edtech-guidance-for-states-and-districts. UK DfE EdTech Testbed, 24 September 2026: rapid evaluations then EEF trials, on outcomes, workload and inclusion. https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/edtech-testbed-programme/edtech-testbed-programme-guidance

## 3. Pitch and demo evidence

- Y Combinator, Geoff Ralston (2016): intrigue, do not close; say what you do plainly; three or four "vertebrae"; one presenter; about seven words per slide; restate key points at the close. https://www.ycombinator.com/blog/guide-to-demo-day-pitches/
- Paul Graham: say what you do in the first sentence; "A demo explains what you've made more effectively than any verbal description"; never have one person talk while operating the computer; no more than four or five numbers; craft a memorable one-liner. https://paulgraham.com/investors.html
- Stripe Atlas, Patrick McKenzie: have a demo ready in 20 seconds; "Give people a link that drops them into a fully authenticated session on a pre-populated account"; fake slow third-party steps openly; know competitors. https://stripe.com/blog/atlas-pitching
- A hackathon judge (July 2026): demos that do not run are the largest score differentiator; opening with context wastes a third of the slot; admitting a weakness raised scores; judges ask "who is this for specifically" and "what breaks first at scale"; keep a 60-second screen recording; "Pick the sentence you want a judge to repeat to another judge an hour later." https://dev.to/kurbaitaev/what-judges-actually-score-notes-from-a-year-of-hackathon-judging-3p4l
- Devpost: the video is one of the first things judges look at, usually under three minutes, elevator pitch first, show the product in use, no slide decks in the video. https://info.devpost.com/blog/6-tips-for-making-a-hackathon-demo-video. MLH: the video should be a demo, not a presentation. https://guide.mlh.com/general-information/judging-and-submissions/judging-plan.md
- Red Gate, 10 September 2026: across about 150 recorded sessions "the demos failed in 2/3 of the sessions"; record every demo and narrate the recording live; never debug live. https://www.red-gate.com/simple-talk/?p=111618

## 4. Commercial viability in university edtech

- Coursera 10-K (February 2026) cites "the complicated approval process within some of these entities" and "longer sales cycles" abroad. https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1651562/000165156226000015/cour-20251231.htm. A vendor guide (Stackmatix, September 2026) puts higher-education cycles at 6 to 12 months, split between department-led purchases and central IT. https://www.stackmatix.com/blog/gtm-for-edtech-startups. No authoritative pilot-to-licence conversion benchmark was found.
- Who competitors sell to: Gray DI targets presidents, CFOs, provosts, deans and institutional research; Coursedog targets provosts, registrars and CIOs; Lightcast's quoted customer is a director of institutional research; Chmura's output is "the case you take to your board or your funder". Nobody sells to the lecturer.
- Pricing anchors (Vendr, secondary): Canvas "$3 to $12 per active user" a year, median contract $21,789. https://www.vendr.com/marketplace/instructure. Coursera for Business $250 to $550 per learner a year. https://www.vendr.com/marketplace/coursera-e79d6. SkillsEngine Team at $999 a month. https://skillsengine.com/. Blackboard's AI Design Assistant is "included as standard" at no extra cost, so AI course generation is already priced at zero inside the LMS. https://www.blackboard.com/ai-design-assistant
- Tool fatigue: WGU Labs faculty survey (2023): 77% feel "always on the job because of technology", 83% still say edtech enhances teaching, only 30% trust their institution to choose the right products, more than half fear edtech reduces autonomy over course design. https://wgulabs.org/posts/survey-higher-ed-faculty-mixed-on-edtech-and-ai-two-thirds-say-higher-ed-is-headed-in-wrong-direction
- Tyton Partners, Time for Class 2026 (June 2026, 3,000 or more respondents): 89% of institutions willing to invest more in faculty-facing AI; faculty daily AI use 25% against administrators 43%; 63% of faculty say they assign real-world projects but only 26% of students report completing one; 60% of students against 70% of administrators believe courses prepare for work; 12% of institutions embed career-connected learning across all undergraduate departments. https://tytonpartners.com/time-for-class-2026/
- EDUCAUSE Horizon Report 2026 (May 2026): leaders put "greater emphasis on alignment with the labor market"; "growing pressure to prove credential ROI"; staffing cuts worsening burnout; "Digital learning is becoming a sustainability strategy"; awareness of AI's environmental cost is growing. https://library.educause.edu/-/media/files/library/2026/5/2026hrteachinglearning.pdf

## 5. Competitors and adjacent products (vendor pages unless noted)

- Lightcast Skillabi (score launched 3 June 2025): program-occupation score 0 to 100 from course descriptions, top-10 missing skills, "18 billion labor market data points", quote-only. Not offered: lecturer-run collection, quoted postings, posting counts, teaching materials. https://lightcast.io/solutions/software/skillabi/align-courses
- Chmura JobsEQ Program Alignment: CIP-to-SOC alignment, "Over 350 million job postings from 45,000+ sites", board reporting, aggregates only. https://www.chmura.com/jobseq/education/program-alignment
- Coursedog: approval workflows plus "Labor Market Insights" inside program proposals; program level, source unnamed. https://www.coursedog.com/products/labor-market-insights
- Watermark Curriculum Strategy: catalogue and approval workflows; no labour-market data on the page. https://www.watermarkinsights.com/discover/lp-curriculum-strategy/
- Anthology Blackboard AI Design Assistant: modules, questions, rubrics and images from existing course context; no labour-market data, no citations. https://www.blackboard.com/ai-design-assistant
- Ellucian Journey (February 2026): labour-market intelligence for continuing-education programmes at 100 or more institutions; learner recommendations, not degree-course design. https://www.ellucian.com/newsroom/ellucian-journey-named-2025-campus-technology-product-year
- Instructure Canvas Career (29 January 2026): skills-first platform for adult and workforce learning. https://www.stocktitan.net/news/INST/instructure-advances-skills-first-workforce-learning-with-broad-w99l8u8l94ks.html
- Coursera for Campus, Credly by Pearson, Riipen, Gray DI, Jobspeaker, SkillsEngine: content, credentials, projects and programme analytics; none at the lecturer's desk.
- Academic signal: UNSW (INTED 2026) mapped CLOs to nine skills with machine learning plus educator review "over 90% accuracy across more than 1,000 courses"; taxonomy mapping, not quoted employer evidence. https://library.iated.org/view/CHUGH2026MAP
- Net gap: no product shows exact posting quotations with honest denominators, lets a lecturer run their own collection, or produces a meeting brief plus an editable teaching pack; all sell to institutional research, provosts or boards.

## 6. Educational significance

- Biggs, constructive alignment: "The key is that all components in the teaching system, the curriculum and its intended outcomes, the teaching methods used, the assessment tasks, are aligned to each other." https://www.biologie.uzh.ch/dam/jcr:94dbcc23-1c4d-4892-bab8-ba16abc4ef66/ConstructiveAlignment_Biggs.pdf
- Limits: Scandurra et al 2023, Studies in Higher Education, 87 papers: the employability-programme evidence base "is dominated by small-scale case studies and evaluations that are not sufficiently robust" to infer causal effects. https://www.uloyola.es/en/scientific-offer/publications/do-employability-programmes-in-higher-education-improve-skills-and-labour-market-outcomes-a-systematic-review-of-academic-literature-article
- Does labour-market information change decisions? Baker, Bettinger, Jacob and Marinescu (NBER 2017): students overestimated salaries by about 13% and underestimated employment probability by almost 25%. https://www.nber.org/papers/w23333. Kerr et al (IZA 2015, 97 Finnish schools): a third of students updated beliefs, but enrolment did not shift. https://www.iza.org/publications/dp/9372. Lesson: information shifts beliefs more than behaviour, so the evidence must arrive with a decision and a deliverable. No study of lecturer-targeted labour-market information changing course decisions was found: a pilot can fill it.
- Employer view, AAC&U Agility Imperative (December 2025, 1,030 employers): 85% say colleges are "doing a good job of preparing students for the workforce"; nine in ten say AI-related skills matter. https://www.aacu.org/research/the-agility-imperative

## Do this in the pitch and the demo

1. Innovation: open with the sentence a judge will repeat an hour later, then name what is new against Skillabi and JobsEQ in 30 seconds.
2. Innovation: put a receipt on screen in the first minute (exact quotation, posting count, date, source), because no competitor page claims this.
3. Technology Readiness: state "software TRL 6" in NASA wording, show the check and smoke suites as evidence, and name the TRL 7 step (one department, one term, inside its LMS).
4. Technology Readiness: demo from a pre-populated, signed-in workspace, mock slow third-party calls and say so, never debug live.
5. Technology Readiness: answer "what breaks first at scale" before they ask (posting coverage, representativeness, model drift); honest limits score.
6. Commercial Viability: anchor against free LMS generation (Blackboard), quote-only institutional data (Lightcast, Chmura) and Canvas at $3 to $12 per user, then propose a per-department subscription that rides existing LMS and data contracts.
7. Commercial Viability: show the demand signal (89% of institutions want to invest in faculty-facing AI; 30% faculty trust in institutional tool choice) and the "no new platform" integration slide; give the 6-to-12-month cycle and a department-led pilot path.
8. Education Significance: frame the teaching pack as constructive alignment: evidence to intended outcomes to aligned activities and assessment, and trace one quotation through all three.
9. Education Significance: be candid that employability evidence is small-scale and that information alone shifts beliefs more than behaviour, so Lyceum pairs evidence with a decision and a deliverable; propose a Tier 4 logic model plus lecturer study, then a Tier 3 before-and-after design.
10. Education Significance: use the perception gap (63% of faculty against 26% of students on real-world projects; 52% underemployed) rather than "universities are failing" (85% of employers say colleges do a good job).
11. Presentation: one presenter, slides as supporting cast, no more than five numbers, restate three points at the close, rehearse until smooth, and keep hard-question answers ready for the two-minute Q&A.
12. Demo: at the booth, split narrator and operator, run one end-to-end path, test on venue wifi with an offline mode, keep the narrated 60-second recording and the video as fallbacks, and bring the university banner the organisers ask for.

Unverified or missing: the official weights, the Presentation and Demo descriptors, any Instructure and Lightcast tie, MLH's criteria page text, IES ESSA tier text, pilot-to-licence conversion norms.

===== FILE: docs/research/design-and-video-references-2026-10-22.md =====
# Research note: Remotion, mascots, honest gamification, dashboards, decks and diagrams

Date: 22 October 2026. References for the overhaul: how to build the demo video in Remotion, what Duolingo's character and motion rules actually say, what the evidence says about gamification for adults, mascot systems in grown-up products, calm dashboard references, deck craft, and how to use product logos in an architecture diagram legitimately. Compiled in a research session on 22 October 2026; dates are publication dates where the page showed one.

## 1. Remotion

- Version: remotion 4.0.537, published 19 October 2026. https://github.com/remotion-dev/remotion/releases
- Licence (v4): free, including commercial videos, for individuals, for-profit organisations with up to three employees, non-profits and evaluation. Educational institutions do not count as non-profits without written permission, so if the university owns Lyceum, check. Company licence otherwise: Creators $25 a seat a month, Automators $0.01 a render with a $100 monthly minimum. The 5.0 terms count the combined headcount of all involved parties; four or more needs a company licence. https://github.com/remotion-dev/remotion/blob/main/LICENSE.md and https://www.remotion.dev/docs/license/terms
- Official skills: "Remotion Agent Skills", `npx skills add remotion-dev/skills`, for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and others; twelve skills including best-practices, create, captions, render, multimedia. Claude Code plugin: `claude plugin marketplace add remotion-dev/claude-code-plugin` then `claude plugin install remotion@remotion`. Any docs URL plus `.md` returns Markdown. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/ai/skills and https://www.remotion.dev/docs/ai/claude-code-plugin. Third-party rule from the house style: scan with SkillSpector before first use.
- Structure: `src/Root.tsx` registers `<Composition>`; every visual is a pure function of `useCurrentFrame()`; scenes via `<Sequence>` and `<Series>`; motion via `interpolate` and `spring`. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/the-fundamentals
- Rendering: `npx remotion render <entry> <compId> out/video.mp4`, H.264 default. On Windows, pass props as a file, not inline JSON. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/cli/render
- Real screens in: Playwright `recordVideo` on `browser.newContext` with `size` equal to the 1920 by 1080 viewport (the file is finalised on `context.close()`), or `page.screenshot`. https://playwright.dev/docs/videos. Put files in `public/` and load with `staticFile()` into `<OffthreadVideo>` or `<Img>`. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/staticfile. Scripted capture beats manual recording because it re-records when the UI changes.
- Captions and voice: `@remotion/captions` with converters from Whisper (`@remotion/install-whisper-cpp`) and ElevenLabs speech-to-text. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/captions. Text-to-speech templates exist (Azure, Google); ElevenLabs TTS has no official package, so generate one file per scene and size scenes with `calculateMetadata`.
- Pitfalls: frames render in parallel, so no CSS transitions, no wall clock, `random()` not `Math.random`; load fonts with `@remotion/fonts` and wait before measuring text; `<Img>` not `background-image`; `delayRender` for async data. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/flickering and https://www.remotion.dev/docs/fonts
- Templates: no official product-demo template; the Recorder template (screen and webcam, Whisper captions) is the closest. https://www.remotion.dev/docs/recorder

## 2. Duolingo design and the gamification evidence

- Building character (November 2020): Duo is geometric, big eyes, detached feet; the human cast shares his shapes; each character has its own correct-answer animation. https://blog.duolingo.com/building-character/
- Reshaping Duo (November 2013): silhouette first, details cannot rescue a weak shape; quarter-turn pose. https://blog.duolingo.com/reshaping-duo/
- Shape language (July 2020): few vector shapes, negative space, exaggeration to lower the fear of mistakes. https://blog.duolingo.com/shape-language-duolingos-art-style/
- World character visemes (November 2022): Rive state machines, 20 or more mouth shapes per character, tiny files. https://blog.duolingo.com/world-character-visemes
- Core tabs refresh (February 2026): character art in every header "felt forced"; ask "does it serve a purpose?"; whitespace over containers. https://blog.duolingo.com/core-tabs-redesign/
- Streak critique: El País (December 2024) on guilt notifications; Desirability Lab (October 2023) "Manipulative, yes. Also completely effective." https://desirabilitylab.com/backfill/backfill-2024-302-duolingo-streak-mechanics
- Evidence: Sailer and Homner 2020, Educational Psychology Review 32, 77-112: g = 0.49 cognitive, 0.36 motivational, 0.25 behavioural, only the cognitive effect robust in rigorous studies. https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10648-019-09498-w. Hamari, Koivisto and Sarsa 2014: effects depend on context and users. Mekler et al 2017: points, levels and leaderboards raised output but not intrinsic motivation. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2015.08.048. Deci, Koestner and Ryan 1999: contingent rewards undermine intrinsic motivation, informational feedback supports it. https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.125.6.627. These justify "quality feedback, no leaderboards, no streaks".
- Ethics: Kim and Werbach 2016, four tests (exploitation, manipulation, harm, character). https://doi.org/10.1007/s10676-016-9401-5. Deterding 2014, "Eudaimonic Design". https://mediarep.org/entities/bookpart/8a83c8bf-8ec2-48c2-bbde-0829488ad549/full

## 3. Mascots in grown-up products

- Mailchimp with COLLINS (September 2018): Freddie reduced to a single-colour silhouette; hand-drawn imperfection. https://www.wearecollins.com/work/mailchimp/
- Notion (Roman Muradov): ink curves, halftone shading, no skin colour, colour only for emphasis. https://designcompass.org/en/2025/01/20/notion/
- GitHub mascots: never as a logo, never for money, security or apologies; "less is more". https://brand.github.com/graphic-elements/mascots
- Slack (Alice Lee, 2017): organic imperfect shapes, darker tones to ground an enterprise brand, 40 or more modular spots. https://www.byalicelee.com/slack
- Headspace with Italic Studio (April 2024): the face gains stress and sadness, a more professional persona for care. https://www.itsnicethat.com/articles/italic-studio-headspace-graphic-design-project-250424
- Consistency rules: one perspective, fixed stroke weights, scale rules per size class. https://www.rmcad.edu/blog/building-an-illustration-system-shapes-strokes-and-scale-rules/. NN/g (April 2024): abstract illustrations are less memorable; keep one cohesive style. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/picture-superiority-effect/
- For the seven agents: one shape family like Duo plus cast, forest ink lines, lime as the single accent, moods through brows and posture rather than colour, and GitHub's rule of keeping mascots out of error, billing and security moments.

## 4. Calm but alive dashboards

- Mercury breakdown (October 2026): green primary, red and yellow reserved for errors, dense but strictly hierarchical. https://www.925studios.co/blog/mercury-design-breakdown
- Robinhood style reference: near-black with one electric lime. https://styles.refero.design/style/48ba2283-13b5-423a-8742-390ea4e53c36
- Linear design refresh (March 2026): "don't compete for attention you haven't earned", "structure should be felt not seen". https://linear.app/blog/behind-the-latest-design-refresh
- Google Material 3 Expressive research (I/O 2025): 46 studies, 18,000 or more participants, key elements spotted up to four times faster; "No amount of emotion can compensate for a lack of clarity." https://design.google/library/expressive-material-design-google-research
- Motion tokens: Atlassian 50 to 150 ms feedback, 150 to 400 ms transitions, reduced-motion support. https://atlassian.design/foundations/motion. Carbon productive against expressive, 70 to 700 ms. https://carbondesignsystem.com/elements/motion/overview/
- Empty states: Polaris, one primary call to action and action-led titles. https://polaris.shopify.com/components/empty-state. Carbon, positive title and next action. https://carbondesignsystem.com/patterns/empty-states-pattern/
- Dribbble, Behance and Mobbin surfaced no strong forest-plus-lime dashboard; Mercury, Linear and Robinhood are the better anchors; amanah-wallet's mascot system maps most closely to Headspace and Mailchimp.

## 5. Deck and poster craft

- Kawasaki 10/20/30. https://guykawasaki.com/the-102030-rule-of-powerpoint/
- Duarte, slide:ology (2008): ideas before slides, diagrams, data, arrangement, movement. https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/slideology/9780596522346/
- Reynolds, Presentation Zen (2008): the Takahashi method, one huge word or number per slide.
- Picture superiority: Shepard 1967 (96.7% against 88.4% recognition); Standing 1973. https://doi.org/10.1080/14640747308400340. NN/g caveats: literal, familiar, cohesive, shown long enough, icons still need labels.
- Mayer, Multimedia Learning: words plus pictures beat words alone in retention and transfer. https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/multimedia-learning/multimedia-principle/C5CE697EE4411F0D8DC0D7B6D6F94EE3

## 6. Architecture diagrams

- C4 notation: title with type and scope, a legend, every line labelled with direction and purpose (never "uses"), technology on lines, consistent colour, legible in black and white. https://c4model.com/diagrams/notation
- Logo sources: Simple Icons is CC0 as a project but each icon keeps its brand licence; read the per-icon guidelines. https://github.com/simple-icons/simple-icons/blob/develop/DISCLAIMER.md. svgl is MIT for the code, logos remain the owners' property. https://github.com/pheralb/svgl
- Brand rules: Microsoft, names referentially fine, logos and icons never without a licence. https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/legal/intellectualproperty/trademarks/usage/general. Google, request form, up to two weeks. https://support.google.com/contact/brand_request_form. Slack media kit. https://slack.com/media-kit. GitHub. https://github.com/logos. Moodle: references to the software are free, logos implying endorsement need permission. https://moodle.com/trademarks/. OpenAI brand page. https://openai.com/brand/. Anthropic has a press kit but no public usage page. Instructure has no public brand page. Safe pattern for a poster: monochrome marks or text labels, "integrates with", no endorsement implied.
- Tools: Excalidraw (MIT), D2 (MPL-2.0, TALA engine for architecture) https://d2lang.com, Mermaid `architecture-beta` with icon packs (layout can overlap) https://mermaid.js.org/syntax/architecture.html, Figma for the print-grade final.

## Recommended Remotion pipeline

1. Install the Remotion agent skills after a SkillSpector scan; pin remotion 4.0.537 across all `@remotion/*`.
2. Write the script as scene JSON (title, agent, on-screen action, line of narration).
3. Drive the real app with Playwright at 1920 by 1080 in demo mode, `recordVideo` per scene plus 2x stills for hero frames, into `public/scenes/`.
4. Convert WebM to MP4 with `npx remotion ffmpeg`, trim, name by scene id.
5. Build `Root.tsx` with one composition per cut; a `<Series>` of scenes, each a `<Sequence>` with `OffthreadVideo` plus a forest and lime overlay layer driven by `spring` and `interpolate`.
6. Load Bricolage Grotesque, Plus Jakarta Sans and JetBrains Mono via `@remotion/fonts` from `public/fonts`, one shared loader awaited before any text measurement.
7. Narration: team voices recorded per line (the dataworks method), text-to-speech only as a fallback; `calculateMetadata` sets scene length from the audio.
8. Captions from the transcript with the script's spelling and stress marks, rendered in the caption band with the speaker's colour bar.
9. Mascot moments: SVG keyframes synced to the frame, one per scene at most, never over an error state.
10. Render locally with a props file (Windows), check determinism by rendering twice, Lambda only if the team grows past the free tier.

## Do not do (the AI-slop tells)

1. No gradient blobs, glassmorphism or glowing lime on everything; lime is one accent per screen.
2. No generic isometric 3D cities or stock "people at laptops" illustrations; one flat ink style, seven characters, the same stroke.
3. No AI-generated text inside graphics, no fake data in screenshots, no mock dashboards; capture the real demo mode and say so.
4. No ragged mixed-logo wall: monochrome marks, equal height, labelled, with a legend.
5. No rapid pop-ins and confetti on every transition; Carbon-style durations and a purpose per motion.
6. No streak counters, leaderboards, badges for volume or guilt notifications anywhere in the UI or the video copy.

